{
 "note": "R94 - the unexplained words of the `ABKC` bank tree (R89 \u00a74): sample-list entry words S (u24) and X (u16), and the 32-bit name hash.\nControlled offline tests only; no meaning is assigned from plausibility. Read-only over the loose trees.\n\nS tests (stream entries): S is identical for the same (bank, record) on PS3 and X360 (platform-independent?); within a list the\n  entries sorted by S give increments dS; dS is compared with num_samples x k for k in {1, 2, 4}, with (num_samples + terminal trim),\n  with the stream byte length, with the block count, and its residue classes (dS mod 2 / 4 / 8 / 30 / 60 / 64) are recorded; the\n  ratio dS / num_samples distribution per (rate, cc); whether S restarts at 0 per list or per bank; whether the S order equals the\n  list order or the record-index order. Ram entries: S vs the 1-based ordinal (R89) re-checked.\nX tests: census by (record type, loop, codec, cc, rate, weight, bank); is X constant within a list? bit decomposition of the large\n  values (0xdf1f, 0xcc34, 0xcf30 ...); X vs the owner class.\nHash tests: (1) is the hash a function of the name (one hash per distinct name across all banks and both platforms)? collisions?\n  (2) is the C table sorted by hash / by name / by node? (3) candidate algorithms x string variants (as-is, lower, upper, without the\n  class suffix, with a trailing NUL) on a TRAINING set of 300 distinct names; any candidate matching >= 95 % is then tested on the\n  HELD-OUT remainder and must predict every held-out pair; every candidate tried is listed with its match count.\nUsage: python r94_abk_sx_hash.py <out.json> [--trees a,b]\n",
